-- Q1: easy / L2 — DHCP automatic IP assignment
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Greenfield Accounting recently expanded its office and added 40 new workstations to its LAN. The IT administrator wants each workstation to automatically receive an IP address when it connects. Which network service fulfills this requirement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)", "Domain Name System (DNS)", "Network Address Translation (NAT)", "Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-123, DHCP automatically assigns IP addresses and related configuration parameters to devices joining a network, eliminating the need for manual IP configuration on each workstation. This is the standard protocol for dynamic address allocation in enterprise LANs.
Wrong (B): DNS resolves domain names to IP addresses but does not assign IP addresses to devices.
Wrong (C): NAT translates private IP addresses to public addresses for internet communication but does not assign addresses to endpoints.
Wrong (D): SNMP monitors and manages network devices but has no role in IP address assignment.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Stateful vs stateless firewall inspection
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Mercer & Daniels LLP is upgrading its network perimeter security. The IT director explains that the new firewall tracks active connections and only allows inbound packets that match an established session initiated from inside the network. Which firewall technology does this describe?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Packet-filtering firewall", "Stateful inspection firewall", "Application-layer proxy firewall", "Circuit-level gateway firewall"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-41 Rev. 1, a stateful inspection firewall maintains a state table of active connections and permits inbound traffic only when it corresponds to an outbound session already established by an internal host. This provides stronger security than simple packet filtering.
Wrong (A): A packet-filtering firewall examines individual packets against static rules (source/destination IP, port) without tracking connection state.
Wrong (C): An application-layer proxy firewall operates at Layer 7 and inspects application-level content, which goes beyond the connection-tracking behavior described.
Wrong (D): A circuit-level gateway monitors TCP handshakes to validate sessions but does not perform deep packet inspection or content-aware filtering.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — DNS purpose
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Which network service translates human-readable domain names into numeric IP addresses?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)", "File Transfer Protocol (FTP)", "Domain Name System (DNS)", "Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-81 Rev. 2, the Domain Name System (DNS) resolves human-readable domain names (e.g., www.example.com) into IP addresses that computers use to route network traffic. DNS is a foundational internet infrastructure service.
Wrong (A): DHCP assigns IP addresses to devices on a network but does not resolve domain names.
Wrong (B): FTP transfers files between systems but has no role in name resolution.
Wrong (D): SMTP handles email transmission between mail servers but does not translate domain names to IP addresses.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Multi-tenancy risk evaluation in cloud
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Ridgepoint Financial Services processes customer credit applications using a SaaS platform hosted in a multi-tenant public cloud environment. During a SOC 2 Type II examination, the service auditor identifies that tenant isolation relies solely on application-level logical controls with no hypervisor-level or network-level segmentation between tenants. The auditor also notes that a recent vulnerability in the shared database engine could have allowed cross-tenant data leakage. Which combination of findings represents the MOST critical risk requiring immediate management attention?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The SaaS vendor should migrate to a private cloud to eliminate all multi-tenancy risks", "The shared database vulnerability should be patched, but logical isolation is sufficient for all environments", "Application-level isolation is adequate if the vendor maintains SOC 2 Type II certification", "The lack of defense-in-depth isolation combined with the database vulnerability creates compounding cross-tenant data leakage risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-144, multi-tenant cloud environments require defense-in-depth with isolation at multiple layers — network, hypervisor, and application. When isolation depends on a single layer and a vulnerability exists in shared infrastructure, the risks compound because one control failure exposes all tenants simultaneously.
Wrong (A): Migrating to a private cloud eliminates multi-tenancy but is disproportionate; layered isolation controls within the existing environment address the root cause.
Wrong (B): Patching alone is insufficient when the architecture lacks defense-in-depth — future vulnerabilities in any single shared component would recreate the same exposure.
Wrong (C): SOC 2 certification does not guarantee adequate technical controls; the report itself identified the isolation weakness.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Load balancer failure scenario
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Harmon Technologies operates a web-based financial reporting portal that uses a single hardware load balancer to distribute requests across four application servers. During quarter-end, the load balancer experiences a hardware failure and the portal becomes completely unavailable despite all four application servers remaining operational. Which infrastructure improvement would MOST effectively prevent this type of outage?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deploy a redundant load balancer pair in an active-passive failover configuration", "Increase the number of application servers from four to eight", "Replace the hardware load balancer with a DNS round-robin configuration", "Add a content delivery network (CDN) in front of the application servers"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-53 CP-2, eliminating single points of failure requires redundancy at every critical component. An active-passive load balancer pair ensures that if the primary fails, the standby immediately assumes traffic distribution without disruption.
Wrong (B): Adding more application servers does not address the single point of failure at the load balancer — all servers remain unreachable if the load balancer fails.
Wrong (C): DNS round-robin lacks health checking and session persistence, creating poor user experience and no automatic failover for downed servers.
Wrong (D): A CDN caches static content at edge locations but does not replace the load balancer function for dynamic application traffic.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — Client-server vs peer-to-peer
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$A small CPA firm allows its five employees to share files by accessing each other''s computers directly, with no central server managing access permissions or logging file activity. Which architecture does this describe, and what is the PRIMARY control weakness?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Client-server architecture; lack of endpoint encryption", "Peer-to-peer architecture; lack of centralized access controls and audit logging", "Hybrid cloud architecture; insufficient network bandwidth", "Mesh network architecture; excessive redundancy"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT 2019 DSS05, a peer-to-peer architecture allows each device to act as both client and server. Without a central server, there are no centralized access controls, authentication, or audit trails — making it unsuitable for environments requiring governance over sensitive data.
Wrong (A): Client-server architecture uses a dedicated server to manage resources; the scenario describes direct device-to-device sharing without a central server.
Wrong (C): Hybrid cloud involves combining on-premises and cloud infrastructure, which is unrelated to the local file-sharing arrangement described.
Wrong (D): A mesh network topology describes how devices are physically or logically interconnected, not how resources are shared and controlled.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — RAID level selection for financial database
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Pemberton Corp stores its general ledger database on a server with six hard drives. The database administrator needs to balance read/write performance with fault tolerance, ensuring the system survives the failure of up to two simultaneous drives without data loss. Which RAID level is MOST appropriate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["RAID 0 (striping with no redundancy)", "RAID 1 (mirroring)", "RAID 6 (dual parity striping)", "RAID 5 (single parity striping)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-209, RAID 6 uses dual distributed parity across all drives, allowing the array to survive up to two simultaneous drive failures without data loss. This makes it well-suited for critical financial databases where fault tolerance is paramount.
Wrong (A): RAID 0 stripes data across drives for performance but provides zero redundancy — any single drive failure results in total data loss.
Wrong (B): RAID 1 mirrors data between drive pairs, which survives one drive failure per mirrored pair but does not efficiently use six drives or guarantee dual-failure tolerance across the array.
Wrong (D): RAID 5 uses single parity and can survive only one drive failure — a second concurrent failure would cause data loss.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Hot site vs warm site vs cold site
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Ashford Financial maintains a disaster recovery facility that has pre-installed servers, networking equipment, and a current copy of the operating environment, but does not have real-time data replication from the production site. In a disaster, the team estimates it would take 4-8 hours to restore the most recent backups before operations resume. Which type of disaster recovery site does this describe?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Hot site", "Reciprocal agreement", "Cold site", "Warm site"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, a warm site has pre-configured hardware and network infrastructure but requires restoration of recent data backups before operations can resume, typically resulting in recovery times of several hours.
Wrong (A): A hot site maintains real-time or near-real-time data replication and can resume operations within minutes, not hours.
Wrong (B): A reciprocal agreement is an arrangement between two organizations to share facilities during a disaster, not a specific site readiness classification.
Wrong (C): A cold site provides only basic facilities (power, space, connectivity) with no pre-installed hardware, requiring days or weeks to become operational.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — MDM controls for mobile devices
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Cortland Advisory Group allows partners to access client tax documents from personal smartphones and tablets. The IT manager implements a solution that enforces device encryption, requires PIN authentication, and can remotely erase all corporate data if a device is lost or stolen. Which technology provides these capabilities?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Mobile Device Management (MDM)", "Virtual Private Network (VPN)", "Data Loss Prevention (DLP)", "Network Access Control (NAC)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-124 Rev. 2, Mobile Device Management (MDM) provides centralized control over mobile endpoints, including enforcing encryption and authentication policies, managing applications, and performing remote wipe of corporate data on lost or compromised devices.
Wrong (B): A VPN encrypts network traffic between the device and corporate network but does not enforce device-level policies like PIN requirements or remote wipe.
Wrong (C): DLP monitors and prevents unauthorized data transfers but does not manage device configuration, encryption enforcement, or remote wipe capabilities.
Wrong (D): NAC controls whether a device can connect to the network based on compliance checks but does not manage the device itself or provide remote wipe functionality.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — WLAN security weakness scenario
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$During an IT infrastructure review at Northbrook Manufacturing, the auditor discovers that the company''s wireless network uses WEP encryption for all Wi-Fi access points, including those in the accounting department where employees process payroll. The IT manager states WEP was chosen because older warehouse scanners are incompatible with newer protocols. Which recommendation BEST addresses the security risk while accommodating the operational constraint?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Disable all wireless networking and require wired connections throughout the facility", "Segment the network into separate SSIDs — WPA2/WPA3 for accounting and a restricted VLAN with WEP for warehouse scanners only", "Keep WEP but add MAC address filtering on accounting access points", "Upgrade all warehouse scanners immediately regardless of cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-153, organizations should use the strongest available wireless security protocol and segment networks to isolate weaker devices. Creating separate SSIDs allows accounting to use WPA2/WPA3 while containing WEP-connected warehouse scanners in a restricted VLAN with limited access.
Wrong (A): Eliminating wireless entirely is operationally impractical and does not balance the warehouse scanner requirement with security needs.
Wrong (C): MAC address filtering is easily bypassed through spoofing and does not compensate for WEP''s fundamental cryptographic weaknesses.
Wrong (D): Immediate replacement may be cost-prohibitive and ignores the need for a risk-based approach that addresses the vulnerability promptly while planning equipment upgrades.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — SLA metrics in cloud contracts
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Weston & Price CPA firm is evaluating cloud providers for its audit management system. The contract specifies 99.95% monthly uptime, meaning the provider guarantees no more than approximately 22 minutes of unplanned downtime per month. What is this contractual guarantee called?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recovery Time Objective (RTO)", "Recovery Point Objective (RPO)", "Service Level Agreement (SLA)", "Business Impact Analysis (BIA)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-146, a Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a contractual commitment between a cloud provider and customer that specifies measurable service guarantees including uptime percentage, performance thresholds, and remedies for non-compliance.
Wrong (A): RTO defines the maximum acceptable time to restore a system after a disruption, not an ongoing uptime commitment.
Wrong (B): RPO defines the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, not service availability guarantees.
Wrong (D): A BIA identifies critical business functions and the impact of their disruption, but it is an internal planning document, not a contractual guarantee from a provider.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — OSI Application layer
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Which OSI layer provides network services directly to end-user applications such as email clients and web browsers?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Transport layer (Layer 4)", "Presentation layer (Layer 6)", "Network layer (Layer 3)", "Application layer (Layer 7)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per the OSI reference model (ISO/IEC 7498-1), the Application layer (Layer 7) is the topmost layer and provides the interface between network services and end-user applications such as HTTP for web browsing and SMTP for email.
Wrong (A): The Transport layer (Layer 4) manages end-to-end data delivery and flow control but does not interact directly with user applications.
Wrong (B): The Presentation layer (Layer 6) handles data formatting, encryption, and compression but does not provide the application-facing network service interface.
Wrong (C): The Network layer (Layer 3) handles logical addressing and routing between networks but operates below the application interface.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — SDN security evaluation
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Kingsley Financial Group is evaluating a migration from traditional networking to Software-Defined Networking (SDN) for its data center. The CISO raises concerns that SDN centralizes the control plane into a single SDN controller, creating a high-value target. The network architect proposes mitigations: (1) deploying redundant SDN controllers in an active-active cluster, (2) encrypting all control-plane communications using TLS, and (3) implementing role-based access controls on the controller management interface. The CISO asks which residual risk remains MOST significant despite these mitigations.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A compromised SDN controller could reprogram network flows across the entire data center in seconds, enabling lateral movement at infrastructure scale", "The encrypted control plane will introduce unacceptable latency for real-time financial transactions", "Active-active clustering eliminates all availability concerns for the control plane", "Role-based access controls on the management interface are unnecessary if TLS encryption is implemented"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-207 and COBIT 2019 DSS05, while redundancy, encryption, and RBAC address availability, confidentiality, and access risks, the fundamental residual risk of SDN is that a compromised controller has centralized authority to modify all network flows simultaneously — enabling an attacker to redirect, intercept, or disrupt traffic across the entire infrastructure at unprecedented speed and scale.
Wrong (B): TLS encryption of control-plane messages introduces negligible latency and does not materially affect data-plane transaction performance.
Wrong (C): Active-active clustering addresses availability but does not eliminate the risk of a compromised controller propagating malicious configurations to all cluster members.
Wrong (D): RBAC and TLS address different threat vectors — RBAC prevents unauthorized administrative actions while TLS protects data in transit; both are necessary.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Subnetting for department isolation
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Barrett Industries has a single Class C network (192.168.1.0/24) shared across its accounting, human resources, and warehouse departments. After a security incident where a warehouse workstation infected with malware spread to the accounting server, the IT team decides to subdivide the network. Which technique allows the IT team to create separate logical networks for each department within the existing address space?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Port forwarding", "Subnetting", "DNS load balancing", "Network Address Translation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-123, subnetting divides a single IP network into smaller logical segments (subnets), each with its own broadcast domain. This enables the IT team to isolate department traffic and apply router-based access controls between subnets, limiting the spread of malware.
Wrong (A): Port forwarding redirects traffic from one port to another and is used to expose internal services externally, not to segment a network into departmental zones.
Wrong (C): DNS load balancing distributes requests across multiple servers using DNS records but does not create separate network segments.
Wrong (D): NAT translates between private and public IP addresses for internet access but does not subdivide an internal network into isolated segments.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — NAT function
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Fielding & Associates CPA firm has 30 internal workstations using private IP addresses (10.0.0.x). When employees access the internet, the router replaces each workstation''s private IP address with the firm''s single public IP address before forwarding the traffic. Which network technology performs this address translation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Virtual Private Network (VPN)", "Domain Name System (DNS)", "Network Address Translation (NAT)", "Quality of Service (QoS)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-77 Rev. 1, Network Address Translation (NAT) maps private internal IP addresses to one or more public IP addresses, allowing multiple internal devices to share a limited number of public addresses for internet access while also obscuring internal network structure.
Wrong (A): A VPN creates an encrypted tunnel for secure communication over a public network but does not translate private addresses to public addresses.
Wrong (B): DNS resolves domain names to IP addresses but does not translate between private and public address ranges.
Wrong (D): QoS prioritizes certain types of network traffic to ensure performance but does not perform address translation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — IPv6 transition considerations
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Oakmont Financial is upgrading its data center network and discovers that several legacy accounting applications only support IPv4. The network architect proposes running both IPv4 and IPv6 simultaneously during the transition so that newer systems can use IPv6 while legacy applications continue on IPv4. Which transition strategy does this describe, and what is the PRIMARY audit concern?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Tunneling; risk of increased latency", "Network segmentation; risk of broadcast storms", "Protocol translation; risk of data loss", "Dual-stack; risk that security controls must protect both protocol stacks simultaneously"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-119, dual-stack operation runs IPv4 and IPv6 concurrently on the same infrastructure. The primary audit concern is that firewalls, intrusion detection systems, and access controls must be configured and maintained for both protocol stacks — a misconfigured IPv6 rule could create an unmonitored attack path.
Wrong (A): Tunneling encapsulates one protocol inside another (e.g., IPv6 within IPv4 packets), which is a different transition mechanism than running both protocols natively.
Wrong (B): Network segmentation divides a network into zones but is not a protocol transition strategy.
Wrong (C): Protocol translation (NAT64/DNS64) converts between IPv4 and IPv6 at a gateway, which is a different approach than running both protocols simultaneously.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — High availability clustering
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Canton Manufacturing runs its ERP system on a single database server. After two unplanned outages in one quarter caused the company to miss financial reporting deadlines, the CIO requests an infrastructure change that enables a standby server to automatically take over ERP operations within seconds if the primary server fails. Which infrastructure approach BEST meets this requirement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Active-passive high-availability clustering with automatic failover", "Nightly full backup to an offsite tape vault", "Deploying the ERP system in a container orchestration platform", "Increasing the primary server memory and CPU capacity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-53 CP-7, active-passive high-availability clustering maintains a synchronized standby node that monitors the primary server via heartbeat signals. If the primary fails, the standby automatically assumes operations within seconds, meeting the requirement for near-instant failover.
Wrong (B): Nightly tape backups protect against data loss but require hours or days to restore, far exceeding the seconds-level failover requirement.
Wrong (C): Container orchestration improves application deployment and scaling but does not inherently provide database-level failover with synchronous replication.
Wrong (D): Upgrading the primary server improves performance but does nothing to address the single point of failure — if the server fails, there is no standby to take over.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — UPS and power redundancy
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Sterling Wealth Advisors installed a device in its server room that provides battery backup power during electrical outages, giving administrators enough time to safely shut down servers and prevent data corruption. Which infrastructure component does this describe?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Power Distribution Unit (PDU)", "Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS)", "Automatic Transfer Switch (ATS)", "Surge protector"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-53 PE-11, an Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) provides short-term battery backup power during outages, protecting equipment from abrupt shutdowns and giving administrators time to perform graceful system shutdowns or transition to generator power.
Wrong (A): A Power Distribution Unit distributes electrical power to rack-mounted equipment but does not store or generate backup power.
Wrong (C): An Automatic Transfer Switch transitions power from utility to generator supply but does not provide instant battery backup during the transition gap.
Wrong (D): A surge protector guards against voltage spikes but does not provide backup power during an outage.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Forward proxy server scenario
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Bancroft Financial deploys a server that intercepts all outbound internet requests from employee workstations, inspects the requested URLs against the company''s acceptable use policy, blocks access to prohibited categories (gambling, social media), and caches frequently accessed content. An auditor is evaluating this control. Which infrastructure component is performing these functions?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Reverse proxy server", "Intrusion prevention system (IPS)", "Forward proxy server", "Web application firewall (WAF)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-44 Rev. 2, a forward proxy server acts as an intermediary for outbound client requests, enabling URL filtering against acceptable use policies, content caching for performance, and logging of employee internet activity for audit purposes.
Wrong (A): A reverse proxy sits in front of internal servers and handles inbound requests from external clients, which is the opposite direction of the scenario described.
Wrong (B): An IPS monitors network traffic for malicious patterns and blocks attacks but does not perform URL categorization or content caching.
Wrong (D): A WAF protects web applications from inbound attacks (SQL injection, XSS) but does not filter or cache outbound employee internet requests.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Star network topology
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Which network topology connects all devices to a single central switch or hub, so that each device has a dedicated link to the center point?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Mesh topology", "Ring topology", "Bus topology", "Star topology"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3, a star topology connects each network device to a central switch or hub through a dedicated point-to-point link. This is the most common LAN topology because it simplifies troubleshooting and isolates individual device failures.
Wrong (A): A mesh topology connects each device to multiple other devices, providing redundant paths but at higher complexity and cost.
Wrong (B): A ring topology connects devices in a circular chain where data passes sequentially from one device to the next.
Wrong (C): A bus topology connects all devices to a single shared communication line (backbone cable), which is largely obsolete in modern networks.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Cloud elasticity vs scalability
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Hartwell Financial experiences heavy traffic on its cloud-hosted tax preparation portal every April but minimal usage the rest of the year. The cloud provider automatically adds compute resources when demand spikes and removes them when demand drops, and the firm only pays for what it uses. Which cloud computing characteristic does this automatic adjustment demonstrate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Elasticity", "High availability", "Fault tolerance", "Load balancing"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-145, elasticity is the capability of a cloud environment to automatically scale resources up or down in response to workload demand, with usage-based billing. This is a defining characteristic of cloud computing that distinguishes it from traditional fixed-capacity infrastructure.
Wrong (B): High availability ensures continuous operation through redundancy but does not specifically describe the dynamic scaling of resources based on demand.
Wrong (C): Fault tolerance is the ability to continue operating despite component failures, not the automatic adjustment of capacity to match workload.
Wrong (D): Load balancing distributes traffic across existing resources but does not add or remove resources based on demand patterns.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Thin client vs thick client audit implications
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Clarendon Investments is replacing desktop PCs running locally installed financial modeling software with thin client terminals that connect to a centralized virtual desktop infrastructure (VDI). During the transition planning review, the auditor assesses the impact on IT controls. Which change in the control environment is MOST significant from an audit perspective?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Thin clients eliminate the need for user authentication", "Centralized VDI shifts endpoint patch management and data storage controls to the data center, reducing the attack surface at each workstation", "Thin clients require higher bandwidth than traditional PCs, increasing network security risks", "VDI eliminates the need for backup and disaster recovery planning"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT 2019 DSS05.03, migrating to VDI centralizes data storage, application management, and patch deployment in the data center. This reduces the attack surface at individual endpoints because no data resides on the thin client itself, simplifying endpoint security controls.
Wrong (A): Thin clients still require user authentication — VDI sessions are protected by the same access controls as traditional environments.
Wrong (C): While thin clients depend on network connectivity, modern VDI protocols are bandwidth-efficient, and this is an operational consideration rather than the most significant control change.
Wrong (D): VDI concentrates resources in the data center, which actually increases the importance of backup and disaster recovery for the centralized infrastructure.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — SNMP network monitoring
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Thornton & Ellis LLP uses a network management system that polls routers, switches, and servers for performance metrics such as CPU utilization, memory usage, and interface errors, and triggers alerts when thresholds are exceeded. Which protocol does this monitoring system most likely use to collect device statistics?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP)", "File Transfer Protocol (FTP)", "Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)", "Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-123, Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) is the standard protocol for network management systems to query devices for operational metrics (CPU, memory, bandwidth, errors) and receive threshold-based alerts (traps) from managed devices.
Wrong (A): HTTP transfers web content between browsers and servers but is not designed for polling network device statistics or receiving operational alerts.
Wrong (B): FTP transfers files between systems but has no capabilities for querying device performance metrics.
Wrong (D): LDAP provides directory services for user authentication and organizational lookups but does not monitor network device performance.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Data center tier classification evaluation
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Pinnacle Capital Management is selecting a colocation provider for its trading platform, which requires 99.995% uptime. The firm evaluates two facilities: Facility A has redundant power and cooling (N+1), dual utility feeds, but a single network path to the internet backbone. Facility B has 2N power and cooling redundancy, multiple independent utility feeds, and diverse network paths, but charges 40% more. The CIO argues that Facility A''s N+1 redundancy is sufficient because the firm has never experienced a power-related outage. Which analysis BEST evaluates this decision?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Facility A is adequate because N+1 redundancy exceeds typical enterprise requirements", "The 40% cost premium eliminates any benefit from Facility B''s additional redundancy", "The CIO''s argument is valid because historical performance predicts future reliability", "Facility A''s single network path and N+1 design cannot meet 99.995% uptime — the single path creates an unmitigated point of failure, and 2N redundancy allows concurrent maintenance without risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per Uptime Institute Tier Standards and NIST SP 800-53 CP-8, achieving 99.995% uptime (Tier IV equivalent) requires fault-tolerant infrastructure with 2N redundancy and no single points of failure. Facility A''s single network path creates an unmitigated failure risk, and N+1 redundancy does not support concurrent maintenance — both are disqualifying for the stated uptime requirement.
Wrong (A): N+1 redundancy supports approximately 99.98% uptime (Tier II/III) but falls short of 99.995% because it cannot sustain concurrent maintenance events.
Wrong (B): Cost-benefit analysis must be weighed against contractual uptime requirements and the financial impact of trading platform downtime, not treated as a standalone disqualifier.
Wrong (C): Past performance does not guarantee future reliability — infrastructure risk assessment must account for potential failure modes, not just historical incident history.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Endpoint protection layered strategy
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Meridian Consulting Group experienced a ransomware incident that bypassed its traditional signature-based antivirus. Management asks the IT director to redesign the endpoint protection strategy. The IT director proposes: (1) deploying Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) that uses behavioral analysis, (2) implementing application whitelisting on all workstations, (3) maintaining the existing signature-based antivirus as a secondary layer, and (4) enabling full-disk encryption on all endpoints. The CFO questions whether all four layers are necessary. Which assessment of the proposed strategy is MOST accurate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All four layers are complementary — EDR detects novel behaviors, whitelisting blocks unauthorized programs, signatures catch known malware, and encryption protects data from physical theft", "Application whitelisting makes EDR redundant since no unauthorized code can execute", "Full-disk encryption prevents ransomware from encrypting files, making the other layers unnecessary", "Signature-based antivirus should be removed since EDR replaces all of its functions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-83 Rev. 1 and COBIT 2019 DSS05.01, defense-in-depth requires multiple complementary controls. Each proposed layer addresses a distinct threat: EDR detects novel attack behaviors, whitelisting blocks unauthorized executables, signatures catch known threats efficiently, and encryption protects data at rest from physical theft — none fully substitutes for another.
Wrong (B): Whitelisting can be bypassed through living-off-the-land attacks using legitimate system tools, so EDR behavioral monitoring remains essential.
Wrong (C): Full-disk encryption protects data from unauthorized physical access but does not prevent ransomware from encrypting files during an active authenticated session.
Wrong (D): Signature-based antivirus efficiently blocks known malware with minimal performance overhead and complements EDR''s behavioral analysis for comprehensive coverage.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: hard / L3 — Routing protocol security
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$During a network infrastructure audit at Drake Financial Services, the auditor discovers that the company''s border routers accept routing updates from neighboring routers without authentication. An attacker who gains access to a connected network segment could inject false routing entries, redirecting financial transaction traffic through a malicious router for interception. Which control MOST effectively mitigates this routing manipulation risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Encrypting all data transmitted across the network with TLS", "Enabling cryptographic authentication on routing protocols so routers only accept updates from verified peers", "Installing an intrusion detection system on the network perimeter", "Implementing a content delivery network to distribute traffic"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-54, enabling cryptographic authentication (e.g., MD5 or SHA-based authentication in OSPF/BGP) on routing protocols ensures that routers only accept routing updates from authenticated, trusted peers. This directly prevents unauthorized injection of false routes.
Wrong (A): TLS encrypts data in transit, protecting confidentiality, but does not prevent an attacker from manipulating routing tables to redirect traffic through a malicious path before it reaches the TLS endpoint.
Wrong (C): An IDS can detect anomalous routing behavior after it occurs but does not prevent the acceptance of unauthorized routing updates.
Wrong (D): A CDN distributes web content for performance but has no relevance to controlling routing protocol security between network routers.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Bandwidth definition
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Which term describes the maximum rate at which data can be transmitted over a network connection in a given time period?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Latency", "Jitter", "Bandwidth", "Throughput"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3, bandwidth refers to the maximum data transfer capacity of a network link, typically measured in megabits or gigabits per second. It represents the theoretical upper limit of data that the connection can carry.
Wrong (A): Latency measures the time delay for a data packet to travel from source to destination, not the data transfer capacity.
Wrong (B): Jitter refers to the variation in latency over time, causing inconsistent packet arrival intervals, not overall capacity.
Wrong (D): Throughput is the actual data transfer rate achieved in practice, which is typically less than the maximum bandwidth due to overhead and congestion.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — OSI Physical layer
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Which OSI layer is responsible for transmitting raw bit streams over physical media such as copper cables, fiber optics, and wireless radio signals?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Data Link layer (Layer 2)", "Network layer (Layer 3)", "Transport layer (Layer 4)", "Physical layer (Layer 1)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per the OSI reference model (ISO/IEC 7498-1), the Physical layer (Layer 1) handles the transmission and reception of raw, unstructured bit streams over physical media. It defines electrical signals, cable specifications, connector types, and radio frequencies.
Wrong (A): The Data Link layer (Layer 2) frames data into packets with MAC addresses and handles error detection, operating above the physical bit transmission.
Wrong (B): The Network layer (Layer 3) handles logical addressing (IP) and routing between networks, not physical signal transmission.
Wrong (C): The Transport layer (Layer 4) provides reliable end-to-end data delivery between applications, operating well above physical media concerns.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — OSI Presentation layer
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Which OSI layer is responsible for data format translation, encryption, and compression between applications?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Presentation layer (Layer 6)", "Session layer (Layer 5)", "Application layer (Layer 7)", "Transport layer (Layer 4)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the OSI reference model (ISO/IEC 7498-1), the Presentation layer (Layer 6) translates data between the format used by the application and the format transmitted over the network. It handles encryption/decryption, character encoding conversion, and data compression.
Wrong (B): The Session layer (Layer 5) manages session establishment, maintenance, and termination between applications but does not handle data formatting.
Wrong (C): The Application layer (Layer 7) provides network services to end-user applications but relies on the Presentation layer for data format translation.
Wrong (D): The Transport layer (Layer 4) manages end-to-end delivery and flow control but does not perform data format translation or compression.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Port security and switch-level controls evaluation
(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture', $EXPL$Eastgate Securities recently discovered that an unauthorized device was connected to an open Ethernet port in a conference room, allowing an attacker to intercept internal network traffic for three days before detection. The IT team proposes three remediation options: (1) enable 802.1X port-based authentication on all switch ports, (2) configure static MAC address filtering on switch ports, or (3) disable all unused switch ports administratively. The CISO asks which approach provides the STRONGEST protection against unauthorized physical network access while remaining operationally sustainable.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Static MAC address filtering provides the strongest protection because MAC addresses uniquely identify devices", "802.1X port-based authentication is strongest because it requires credential-based verification before granting any network access, and it scales with centralized RADIUS management", "Disabling unused ports is sufficient because it eliminates all physical access points", "Combining MAC filtering with disabled ports eliminates the need for 802.1X authentication"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 and IEEE 802.1X, port-based network access control requires devices to authenticate via credentials (certificates or username/password) through a RADIUS server before receiving any network access. Unlike MAC filtering, 802.1X cannot be bypassed by spoofing a hardware address, and centralized RADIUS management scales across all switch infrastructure.
Wrong (A): MAC addresses can be easily spoofed by an attacker, making static MAC filtering a weak control that provides no real protection against a determined adversary.
Wrong (C): Disabling unused ports addresses only one vector and creates operational friction — ports must be re-enabled each time a legitimate device is connected, and it does not protect active ports from unauthorized substitution.
Wrong (D): MAC filtering is inherently bypassable through spoofing, so combining it with disabled ports does not achieve the credential-based verification that 802.1X provides.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;